# FFmpeg Video Generation — Strict CPU & RAM Resource Isolation Plan

Modify the timelapse video generation engine and background job infrastructure to guarantee server stability, controlled RAM usage, strict CPU caps below 50%, and job concurrency protection.

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Key Infrastructure & Behavior Decisions:**
> 1. **Dedicated Celery Worker (`video` Queue)**: Video generation tasks will be routed to a dedicated Celery worker service (`rawadh_celery_video`) running with `--concurrency=1`. This isolates video processing from API tasks and general background workers.
> 2. **Job Locking**: Simultaneous video generation requests for the same camera/timelapse will be guarded by a Redis lock. If a job is already in progress, duplicate jobs will log a warning and exit gracefully without overloading the server.
> 3. **CPU Priority (`nice`) & Docker CPU Cap**: FFmpeg processes will run with OS priority `nice -n 10` (or configured value) and container CPU limits (`cpus: "0.50"`), ensuring normal server processes always take priority.
> 4. **Longer Execution Times**: Video creation will intentionally take longer to complete to preserve CPU and RAM stability.

